# Git

A question is not permission. `reset`, `rebase`, `push --force`, `push --force-with-lease`, `branch -D`, `clean`, `checkout -- <path>`, and `worktree remove --force` run only on an order that names the operation and the target SHA or ref. That holds for the agent's own uncommitted edit too: undo it with an edit. A worktree with changes is removed after its changes are undone with an edit or committed, so that `git worktree remove` needs no `--force`.

This holds for a conflict that another PR's merge caused: ask how to solve it (rebase and force-push, or merge `main` into the branch) before running either.

When asked about such an operation, answer with the current `HEAD` SHA, the target SHA, what would disappear, and the exact command. Then wait.

- Finished work goes to a PR in the same turn, without waiting for an ask: commit on the Issue's branch, push it, and open the PR. The owner reviews on the PR, from wherever they are; uncommitted changes on this machine cannot be reviewed. Do not stop at "may I commit?".
- Long work runs in an agent worktree (`git worktree add .dev/<branch> <branch>`), not in the main working tree: the owner may switch that tree to another branch to review, and edits made after the switch land on the wrong branch.
- A scratch worktree (to build an earlier commit, to measure) is removed without `--force`: delete its build output (`bin/`, `obj/`) first, then `git worktree remove <path>`. If git still refuses, the worktree holds changes; look at them before anything else.
- When the PR's branch is checked out in the owner's tree, git does not let a worktree check it out too. Work on a temporary branch in the worktree and push it to the PR's branch (`git push origin tmp/16-select:feat/16-select`). Do not switch or reset the owner's tree; say in the reply that its branch is behind until the owner pulls.

```text
# BAD — git switch feat/16-select in the owner's tree, or a commit there while the owner reviews
# GOOD — git switch -c tmp/16-select feat/16-select in .dev/wt; commit; git push origin tmp/16-select:feat/16-select
```
- This covers the Issue's own branch only. Never commit to `main` or push to it, and never merge.
- `git branch --show-current` is part of the command that commits. A chain that creates a branch can fail before it does, and the commit then lands on `main`.
- A push that follows a local test is chained to it so that a failed test stops the push (`./build.ps1; if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) { git push }`). CI is the merge gate; the local run only keeps a known failure from reaching it.
- When the gate fails only on a test that an open Issue tracks and the PR does not touch, the push still stops. The gate stops at the first failing step, so run the remaining test projects by hand; then write the failed test and its Issue under Verification and ask the owner whether to push.
- Read `gh pr view <n> --json state` before pushing to a branch that has a PR. A push to the branch of a merged PR recreates the deleted branch and reaches nothing.
- If history was rewritten, put the SHA from before the rewrite on the first line of the reply.
- Never skip hooks (`--no-verify`) or signing unless the owner orders it.
